I've committed the fix as 745b3c73. Build, tests, clippy and fmt all pass. I couldn't post the step-4 comment on #869: gh returns HTTP 401: Bad credentials, so it needs to be posted by hand. The text is at the bottom.

What I found first. I confirmed the problem by reading the code, not with a live run, because the confirmation prompt needs a terminal and a model. The bash tool copied permissions.allow into its approval check once, when the session started, and /cd never touched it. So if a trusted launch project allowed cargo *, cargo build kept running without asking in any directory /cd reached. Meanwhile the same /cd's "trust is now OFF" message said the project's permissions.allow "will not run here", which wasn't true. The problem was real, so this isn't the "nothing to fix" case.

Where the fix lives. It's inside the check itself, PermissionConfig::check, which now skips any allow pattern that /cd has suspended. Both places that read the allow list (bash commands and file write approval), plus any added later, get the change without extra wiring. All the new logic is in a new file, src/cd_allow.rs.

How it decides what to suspend. Permissions come from one source per session: --allow/--deny flags replace the config entirely, and only one config file wins. So the launch project's patterns are recorded once at startup, as the active allow list whenever no flag was given and the winning config was the project's own. On each /cd:
• **Inside the launch project** (its directory or anything below it), nothing is suspended, so going away and coming back restores the patterns.
• **Anywhere else**, every launch-project pattern is suspended, except ones your own ~/.yoyo.toml or ~/.config/yoyo/config.toml also lists.
• **--allow flags** are never suspended.
• **`/cd` prints** one line naming what it suspended.
• **`/permissions`** shows suspended patterns under their own "Suspended allow patterns" heading, not under the ✓ list.

The new directory's own [permissions] allow still isn't applied, because that would widen permissions and needs the trust gate first.

Tests. All use stores passed in as parameters rather than the process-wide one. They cover:
• a launch-project pattern is dropped after /cd
• a pattern also in your own config survives
• flags and user-config sessions record nothing to suspend
• a sibling directory like /work/ab doesn't count as inside /work/a
• the round trip restores the patterns
• with nothing suspended, results are identical to the old check across a table of commands
• the /permissions text matches exactly

For the positive control, I disabled the filter, the drop test failed by name, and it passed again once restored, all in one command. No NEUTERED marker remains.

Other changes:
• `config.rs (+5 lines) and cli.rs (+1) were already at the edge of their size limits, so I updated both entries in tests/module_size.rs` with the reason.
• I updated `docs/src/configuration/permissions.md and docs/src/usage/commands.md`, and added an ARCHITECTURE.md entry.
• **Not fixed:** when /cd turns trust ON, the message says the new directory's permissions.allow is "live here". No /cd code makes that true.

Comment for #869:
│ Day 229, 745b3c73. Before the fix (found by reading the code, no live run): the bash tool copied permissions.allow once at startup and /cd never touched it, so a trusted launch project's allow kept auto-approving commands in any directory /cd reached, while the trust-OFF message said it "will not run here". Fixed: PermissionConfig::check now skips the launch project's patterns outside its tree. Patterns your own config also lists and --allow flags still apply. /cd back into the project restores them. /permissions lists them as suspended. Still not applied by /cd: the new directory's [permissions] allow, hooks, MCP servers and skills, plus the 2 UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS rows (src/context.rs, src/skill_fence.rs). Also seen: the trust-ON message says the new directory's permissions.allow is "live here", which nothing makes true.
